{
 "id": "2025-peng-chongqing",
 "name": "Chongqing Pocket Parks: Scenic Beauty Estimation (SBE)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Peng & Afla (2025), \"A Multi-Dimensional Assessment of Pocket Park Landscapes: Insights from Scenic Beauty Estimation and Analytic Hierarchy Process in Dadukou District, Chongqing\", Sustainability 17:2020. Participants say whether they are landscape professionals, then rate the scenic beauty of 18 eye-level photographs (three per park) of six pocket parks in Dadukou District, Chongqing (Vegetable Commune, Xingfuli Park, Happy Yuli Garden, Longqiao Pocket Park, Huangjue Memory Park, Steel Park) on a seven-point scale, one at a time in random order. Analysis: Scenic Beauty Estimation, standardising each respondent's ratings (z-scores) and averaging per photograph and per park; compare professionals with non-professionals. In the original study (97 valid questionnaires, 48 landscape professionals and 49 non-professionals, August 2024), Happy Yuli Garden scored highest (mean SBE 0.352) and Longqiao Pocket Park lowest (−0.026); spatial layering, plant diversity, landscape harmony and colour richness explained most of the variation (R² = 0.897).\n\nPhotographs: the paper's Supplementary Figure S1 (taken by the authors in July 2024; article CC BY 4.0), used with credit; the template is removed if the authors object.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) the paper gives only \"a seven-point scale to assess scenic beauty\"; the question wording and the end-point labels (1 = very low, 7 = very high) are our own; (2) the photographs are shown one at a time in random order; the paper does not describe presentation order or format; (3) the second part of the study (expert AHP weighting of 28 landscape indicators on a 1–9 scale, without photographs) is not included; (4) the supplement photographs are about 485 × 333 pixels, smaller than the original questionnaire images may have been; (5) an online consent checkbox was added.",
